Wix is read-only, and Bryan's wording wins
Two standing orders, in a new root `CLAUDE.md` so agents load them
automatically instead of re-deriving them wrongly next session.

## Wix is a READ-ONLY interface. No exceptions.

Your words, and I've written it as strongly as you asked — including the
"regardless of what permissions it offers" part, which is the bit that
actually does the work. The connector may well expose write scopes; the file
says plainly that an OAuth grant is not a decision anybody made.

The reasoning I wrote down, so it survives without you: Wix is the *live
public site*, so a write is not staging — it's what visitors see immediately.
Nothing there is under version control, so none of this repo's safeguards
apply and we cannot revert our own mistake. And divergence is the real hazard
mid-migration: once both sites are edited, they disagree, and the old one wins
because it's the one actually published.

It's explicit that **reading is the whole job anyway** — including your live
errand, pulling the old photographs of the spaces that Bryan thinks are still
up there. If something genuinely must change on Wix, the order is: say so and
stop, a human does it in the admin.

## Bryan's wording is authoritative

Also your call, and I think it's right. The file says don't paraphrase him
into our voice, don't re-expand what he shortened, fix a typo and keep his
sentence. It quotes his reserve-page intro as the worked example of why —
more said, fewer words.

It turns out the repo had already reached this conclusion on its own, in the
header of `_data/facilities.yml`: *"His words are kept close to verbatim —
this is the one part of the site not written by us, and it reads better for
it."* I cited that rather than inventing a new justification.

**One carve-out, deliberately.** Where his wording changes *policy* rather
than prose, it stops being a copy question and comes to you. The credit-card
line is the worked example in the file — "temporary charge as collateral" vs
"valid credit card for late fees and incidentals" are different arrangements,
not two phrasings of one. Without that carve-out, "his wording is
authoritative" would quietly let an agent change a policy because it was
better written.

## Also

`CLAUDE.md` is added to `exclude:` in `_config.yml` in this same commit, and
the file makes that a rule for any future internal doc — a `.md` without front
matter otherwise gets served at a public URL, which is how MANIFEST, REDIRECTS
and RESERVE-DESIGN each leaked once.

## Verified

`_config.yml` parses and both `CLAUDE.md` and `REVIEW-NOTES.md` are in
`exclude` — Ruby's YAML loader. A full `jekyll build` is now genuinely
runnable again on this machine (see the toolchain PR); I have not re-run it
here because this branch changes no template or page.

## Not done

The Wix connector is **not usable from this session** — it appears in the MCP
list now but is unauthenticated, and this session can't run the OAuth flow.
Nothing here depends on it; the order is written for when it works.
